@startuml proto-ds18b20-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-ds18b20-frame.puml - (b) the DS18B20 command and scratchpad layout,
' and the two slot grammars that carry it.
'
' A DS18B20 has a 64-bit ROM code (a family code, a 48-bit serial number and a
' parity bit) and a nine-byte scratchpad. This act addresses one sensor with
' SKIP ROM, so the ROM code never appears on the wire - and that is worth saying
' in the figure, because "no ROM code" is a decision and not an absence.
' ===========================================================================

title DS18B20 - the bytes on the wire, and the two slot grammars\n<font size=12>SKIP ROM means the 64-bit ROM code is never sent; bits are LSB first</font>

left to right direction
hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<g>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFF4D6
  BorderColor #C8B800
}
skinparam rectangle<<w>> {
  BackgroundColor #DCE6F5
  BorderColor #5577AA
}
skinparam rectangle<<r>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam rectangle<<off>> {
  BackgroundColor #EFEFEF
  BorderColor #999999
}

rectangle "RESET\n\n<b>485.7 us</b> of LOW\nhost-driven\n\nfloor: <b>480 us</b>" as A <<g>>
rectangle "PRESENCE\n\n<b>120.0 us</b>\nSENSOR-driven\n\nband: 60 .. 240 us\n\n<b>the device initiates</b> - it is the\nonly act in the family where\nthe far end speaks first" as B <<g>>
rectangle "0xCC  SKIP ROM\n\n<b>8 write slots</b>\n\none addressed sensor,\nso the 64-bit ROM code\nis NEVER sent" as C <<w>>
rectangle "0xBE  READ SCRATCHPAD\n\n<b>8 write slots</b>\n\nthen 16 read slots\nfollow, unattended" as D <<w>>
rectangle "SCRATCHPAD BYTE 0\n\n<b>0x2B</b> = <b>43</b>\nthe temperature,\nLSB byte first\n\n8 read slots" as E <<r>>
rectangle "SCRATCHPAD BYTE 1\n\n<b>0x01</b>\n\n8 read slots\n\nbytes 2..8 exist and are not\nread: the loop is the same for\neach and sixteen slots\ndemonstrate the SLOT TIMING as\nwell as seventy-two, at a third\nof the simulated time. The byte\ncount is one number in dmem[3]." as F <<r>>
rectangle "PARK\n\ndmem[14] = 1" as G <<off>>

A -[#3A7]-> B
B -[#3A7]-> C
C -[#3A7]-> D
D -[#3A7]-> E : 8 read slots
E -[#3A7]-> F : 8 more
F -[#3A7]-> G

note bottom of A
  <b>The reset is COUNTED, not waited for.</b>

  Nothing on the wire announces that 480 us is up, so
  it is the delay routine's job - OW_RST = 58 on the
  (4,40) pair, 485.5 us nominal, 485.7 measured.
end note

note bottom of B
  <b>The presence is WAITED FOR, twice.</b>

  Two spin loops, and no counted delay between them:
  the pulse's length is the sensor's to choose
  (60-240 us) and the host cannot know it. If no
  sensor is there the first loop simply never exits,
  which is the correct behaviour for a bus with no
  timeout in this ISA - and the testbench
  distinguishes "no sensor" from "sensor present"
  by whether the program got past this point.
end note

note top of C
  <b>Why 0xCC and not a 64-bit ROM code.</b>

  A ROM match is the addressable case: the host sends
  all eight ROM bytes, the sensor compares them with its
  own, and only the addressed one answers. With one
  sensor on the bus that is 64 slots to learn nothing,
  and it is the case that costs 64 slots of simulation.

  SKIP ROM is 8 slots and is exactly right here. What
  it costs is stated rather than glossed: with it, a bus
  with two sensors would have BOTH answer, and the two
  read streams would collide. The act claims the
  single-sensor case and says so.
end note

note bottom of D
  <b>Why the two halves of the exchange need different sample idioms.</b>

  A write slot: the HOST pulls the line low. A 1 is a
  short low then a release; a 0 is the low held for the
  whole slot.

  A read slot: the SENSOR holds the line. A 0 leaves it
  released for the rest of the slot; a 1 pulls it low
  again for 1-15 us after the initiation pulse.

  So in the read direction a ONE is LOW and in the
  write direction a ZERO is LOW. The read path first
  borrowed the write path's test and both bytes came
  back exactly complemented - 0x2b as 0xd4, 0x01 as
  0xfe. A sensor that transmits its data inverted still
  transmits DATA.
end note

legend bottom
  |= direction |= a 0 is |= a 1 is |= host's job |=
  | <b>WRITE</b> (host -> sensor) | the line LOW for the WHOLE slot, ~60 us | a SHORT low (1-15 us) then RELEASE for the rest | drive low, and <b>CLAIM the pin first</b> |
  | <b>READ</b> (sensor -> host) | the line RELEASED for the rest of the slot | the line PULLED DOWN again for 1-15 us | release, then <b>SAMPLE THE LEVEL</b> at a fixed instant |

  <b>THE BITS ARE LSB FIRST</b>, which is the opposite of the DHT11 and of the
  WS2812. The write path needs no shift at all - it masks the byte's own bit 0,
  sends, and rotates the byte right so the next bit 0 is the next bit of the
  frame. The read path shifts the accumulator <b>right</b> with the new bit at
  the top, so the first bit read slides down to bit 0 by the eighth.

  Copying the DHT11's shift-LEFT + bit-at-LSB here is the defect the mutation
  case ow-bit-order pins: every byte comes back REVERSED, with the right bit
  count and the wrong values.
end legend

@enduml
